---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CallToAction from '~/components/widgets/CallToAction.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Relapse & Recovery | Quit Smoking Hub',
  description:
    'Lapse vs relapse, why relapses happen, what to do after one, and how community experience plus research can help you get back on track — without shame.',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <section class="relative mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 pt-8 md:pt-12 pb-4">
    <p class="text-base font-bold tracking-wide uppercase text-secondary mb-2 text-center">Relapse &amp; Recovery</p>
    <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold leading-tighter tracking-tighter font-heading text-heading text-center">
      A relapse is not the end of your quit
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl mx-auto text-lg text-muted text-center">
      Most successful quitters made multiple attempts. A relapse is information — about triggers, timing and
      situations — not a verdict on your character or your chances.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 sm:px-6 py-8 space-y-10">
    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-3">Lapse vs relapse</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold font-heading text-xl mb-2">Lapse</h3>
          <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 text-sm">
            A single slip — one cigarette or a few — followed by returning to not smoking. Lapses are common and do
            not erase progress, but they should be taken seriously: a lapse unmanaged often becomes a relapse.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card p-6">
          <h3 class="font-bold font-heading text-xl mb-2">Relapse</h3>
          <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 text-sm">
            A return to regular smoking. Relapse is a normal phase in the quitting process for many people, not a
            failure of willpower. Research treats relapse as a predictable risk to plan for, not a moral event.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-3">What commonly happens before a relapse</h2>
      <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 mb-3">
        Research and community reports point to similar risk situations. This list is not a prediction — it is a
        planning checklist:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
        <li>Drinking alcohol or being at a party with smokers</li>
        <li>High-stress events (work crisis, conflict, bad news)</li>
        <li>Strong negative emotions: anger, loneliness, boredom</li>
        <li>Overconfidence after a milestone ("one won't hurt")</li>
        <li>Stopping medication or support too early</li>
        <li>Being around close friends or family who smoke</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-3">What to do after a lapse or relapse</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-3 text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
        <li><strong class="text-heading">Stop again immediately.</strong> Remove remaining cigarettes now.</li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-heading">Reconstruct what happened:</strong> What was the trigger? What time, place and
          emotional state? Who was there? Was alcohol involved?
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-heading">Write down the lesson</strong> — one specific change for next time (e.g.
          "no alcohol for two more weeks", "leave the party early").
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-heading">Set a new quit date</strong> within days, not months.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-heading">Review your method:</strong> would medication, NRT or structured support
          strengthen this attempt?
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading mb-3">What the relapse data shows</h2>
      <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400">
        The highest relapse risk is in the first weeks after quitting; risk declines substantially over the first
        year. Each additional quit attempt raises the cumulative chance of eventual success. Medication and
        behavioral support reduce relapse rates in trials. Our structured relapse database extracts the same
        variables from community reports — what happened before, the trigger, the context, the outcome — so patterns
        can be seen across many attempts, without judgment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card p-6">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold font-heading mb-2">Language matters</h2>
      <p class="text-muted dark:text-slate-400 text-sm">
        You will not find "weak", "failure" or "lack of willpower" on this site. Relapse, return to smoking, quit
        attempt, recovery — these describe the process without judging the person.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CallToAction
    title="Ready to try again?"
    subtitle="Rebuild your quit plan with what you learned."
    actions={[
      { variant: 'primary', text: 'Start Here', href: '/start/' },
      { text: 'Compare Quit Methods', href: '/methods/' },
    ]}
  />
</Layout>
